## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical experience designing observable, scalable Azure telemetry pipelines with proper instrumentation, tenant isolation, and comprehensive monitoring integration for enterprise-grade media analytics platforms.

## Key Concepts

- OpenTelemetry integration across Event Hubs, Databricks, and Delta Lake
- End-to-end trace correlation and structured logging
- Adaptive sampling strategies and retention policies
- Tenant-level RBAC and data governance
- Comprehensive monitoring with Application Insights and Azure Monitor
- Real-time alerting and SLA management

## Code Example

```javascript
// Propagate correlation across services
const traceHeader = appInsights.getCorrelationContext();
const traceId = traceHeader.traceId;

// Instrument Event Hubs producer
eventHubProducer.send({
  body: eventData,
  properties: {
    traceId: traceId,
    tenantId: tenantId,
    region: region,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
  }
});

// Configure OpenTelemetry sampling
const sampler = new ParentBasedSampler({
  root: new TraceIdRatioBasedSampler(0.01) // 1% production
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design privacy-preserving ML pipelines across tenants with governance constraints. It also probes how to coordinate training, model registry, and DR while controlling cost.

## Key Concepts
- Federated learning across tenants
- Azure ML orchestration and Delta Lake
- Purview RBAC BYOK governance
- Drift monitoring and auditability
- DR and cost optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for federated training loop
function train_round(tenant_config, local_data_uri, model, aggregator_uri){
  const local_model = train(model, load(local_data_uri));
  const update = compute_update(local_model, model);
  send_update_to_server(aggregator_uri, update);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect and respond to drift across tenants?
- How would you handle non-IID data and stragglers in the federation?

## Why This Is Asked

